        "e": "<strong>[Governing Principle: Unstated Presupposition of Ethnogenetic Continuity]</strong> Linking an ancient political entity to modern ethnic groups requires the assumption that linguistic, genetic, or cultural transmission persisted across the intervening millennia.<br><br><em>Step-by-Step Derivation:</em><br>1. <em>Identify the Claim</em>: In Paragraph 1, the author asserts: 'The Hun’s Union in Central Asia contributed to the later emergence of the Kazakh nation and other Turkic peoples.'<br>2. <em>Identify the Gap</em>: The passage also states that Attila's empire rapidly collapsed in 453 AD. How can a short-lived, collapsed confederation contribute to modern nations that formed centuries later?<br>3. <em>Deduce the Bridge</em>: The author must assume that the social, linguistic, and cultural fabric of the Central Asian tribes survived the political dissolution of the Hunnic state.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A (Direct Factual Inversion): The passage explicitly notes the empire collapsed in 453 AD; its political borders did not survive.<br>&bull; B (Contradicted by Text): Paragraph 2 explicitly highlights the 'inability to restrain dozens of tribes and entities that were not related to the Huns socially and ethnically.'<br>&bull; C (Extraneous Homogeneity): Asserts that all nomads shared identical creeds, a sweeping assumption unnecessary to ethnogenetic continuity."
