        "e": "<strong>[Governing Principle: Experimental Discrimination of Social vs Non-Social Triggers]</strong> In evolutionary psychology, an emotional adaptation must demonstrate functional specificity: it must fire selectively to socially threatening interlopers rather than indiscriminate novelty.<br><br><em>Step-by-Step Derivation:</em><br>1. <em>Data Interrogation</em>: Paragraph 5 details the three conditions: (1) realistic stuffed dog, (2) novel jack-o'-lantern pail, (3) children's pop-up book.<br>2. <em>Methodological Logic</em>: The author states: 'This enabled us to test whether the elicitation of jealousy required that the owner show affection to an appropriate stimulus (what appeared to be a conspecific) or whether affectionate behaviors directed to a nonsocial stimulus would be enough.'<br>3. <em>Conclusion</em>: The pail was a nonsocial control to verify whether jealousy requires an interloper that looks like a conspecific rival.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A (Confounding Variable Mismatch): Auditory stimulation was tested by the melody-playing children's book, not the silent jack-o'-lantern pail.<br>&bull; B (Cognitive Overreach Trap): The entire hypothesis of primordial jealousy is that it does NOT require advanced theory-of-mind or complex social cognition.<br>&bull; D (Absurd Semantic Inversion): The test was not comparing canine love for toys vs humans, but whether toys could trigger jealousy when given human affection."
